>> title: Turnip Cake
>> category: Flatbreads & Pancakes
>> tags: daikon, rice flour, chinese, steamed, pan-fried
>> counters: Dim Sum Counter
>> aka: lo bak go, lo baak gou, luo bo gao, 蘿蔔糕, radish cake, daikon cake, fried radish cake
>> servings: 12
>> time: 14 hr
>> step.1: fry the shrimp, sausage and mushroom 5 min
>> step.2: simmer the grated daikon 15 min, keep the liquor
>> step.3: stir the hot daikon into the slurry, fold the savouries through
>> step.4: steam 50 min, cool 1 hr, chill 12 hr
>> step.5: pan-fry the slices 8 min, turning once

Fry @dried shrimp{1/4%cup}(25 g; soaked 20 min and chopped), @lap cheong{2%links}(Chinese cured sausage, finely diced) and @dried shiitake mushrooms{4}(soaked and diced) in @peanut oil{1%Tbs} in a #wok{} for ~fry{5%min}, until the kitchen smells of the sausage.

Simmer @daikon{2%lb}(900 g; peeled and coarsely grated) with @water{1/2%cup}(120 mL), @kosher salt{1%tsp}, @sugar{1%tsp} and @white pepper{1/2%tsp} in a covered #pot{} for ~simmer{15%min}, until it slumps and goes translucent. Do not drain it — that liquor is most of the water in the batter, and all of the flavour.

Stir @&(~1)hot daikon{} into @rice flour{1 1/2%cups}(210 g) slaked smooth with @water{1/2%cup}(120 mL), then fold @&(~2)shrimp and sausage{} through. The daikon has to go in hot; that is what thickens the batter enough to hold the loaf up.

Steam @&(~1)batter{} in an oiled 8-in #cake pan{} in a #steamer{} for ~steam{50%min}, until a skewer comes out clean, then ~cool{1%hr} and ~chill{12%hr} overnight.

Slice @&(~1)set loaf{} into 1/2-in slabs and pan-fry them in @peanut oil{2%Tbs} in a #skillet{} for ~fry{8%min}, turning once, until both faces have a crust and the middle is soft.

Chill the loaf whole and it goes home in a box to be sliced and fried across the week; fry the slices now and they come to the table with oyster sauce and chilli. The shop sells it at both stages and neither one is the unfinished version. There is no turnip anywhere in it.
